Given a 128-bit header split into hi and lo, pack four fields into non-overlapping bit ranges: A (bits 0-7), B (bits 8-19), C (bits 32-47) in lo, and D (bits 80-111) in hi. Implement:

void packHeader128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint8_t A, uint16_t B, uint16_t C, uint32_t D, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo);

Use only bitwise operations (shifts, AND, OR, NOT); no loops or conditionals. Provide the function signature, a brief justification, plus 1-2 quick examples?

questions.explanation:
## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of 128-bit bit packing, non-overlapping fields, and in-place updates without loops. It exercises careful mask calculation and cross-boundary writes.

## Key Concepts
- Bit masks and clearing specific ranges
- Cross-boundary placement across lo/hi
- Safe in-place updates without branching
- Endianness-agnostic bit numbering

## Code Example
```c
void packHeader128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint8_t A, uint16_t B, uint16_t C, uint32_t D, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  uint64_t lo_mask = ((uint64_t)0xFF) | (((uint64_t)0xFFF) << 8) | (((uint64_t)0xFFFF) << 32);
  uint64_t hi_mask = ((uint64_t)0xFFFFFFFF) << 16;
  *out_lo = (lo & ~lo_mask) | (uint64_t)A | (((uint64_t)B) << 8) | (((uint64_t)C) << 32);
  *out_hi = (hi & ~hi_mask) | (((uint64_t)D) << 16);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if ranges could overlap and you needed deterministic behavior?
- How would you validate correctness across all inputs in a unit test?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to reason about cross-half bit patterns and leverage a reversal primitive to detect palindromicity without loops.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit representations split across two 64-bit halves
- Cross-half bit equality, palindrome concept
- Using an existing reversal primitive to avoid per-bit work

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
int isPalindrome128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t hi_rev, lo_rev;
  reverse128(hi, lo, &hi_rev, &lo_rev);
  return (hi == hi_rev && lo == lo_rev) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- Could you implement a pure bitwise variant that does not rely on reverse128?
- How would you generalize to 256-bit values with four halves?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit data as four independent 32-bit lanes and perform lane-wise rotation without loops. Demonstrates precise bit-slicing, correct boundary handling, and minimal, provable code paths.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit packing into 4×32 lanes
- Lane-wise rotation using (x<<n) | (x>>(32-n))
- Bit masking to confine intermediate results to 32-bit lanes
- Clamping n with & 31 to handle n >= 32

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64 lo; uint64 hi; } uint128;

uint128 rotateWithinLanes128(uint128 x, unsigned n) {
    n &= 31;
    uint32 a = (uint32)(x.lo);
    uint32 b = (uint32)(x.lo >> 32);
    uint32 c = (uint32)(x.hi);
    uint32 d = (uint32)(x.hi >> 32);

    x.lo = ((uint64)((a << n) | (a >> (32 - n))) << 32) |
           ((b << n) | (b >> (32 - n)));
    x.hi = ((uint64)((c << n) | (c >> (32 - n))) << 32) |
           ((d << n) | (d >> (32 - n)));
    return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests deep knowledge of bit manipulation techniques for combinatorial enumeration without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Next higher number with same popcount (snoob algorithm)
- Handling 64-bit overflow with masking
- Pure bitwise operations and shifts only
- Edge cases are constant-time checks

## Code Example
```javascript
function nextPermutation64(v) {
  const MASK = (1n << 64n) - 1n;
  if (v === 0n || v === MASK) return 0n;
  const c = v & -v;
  const ripple = v + c;
  const ones = v ^ ripple;
  const next = ripple | ((ones >> 2n) / c);
  return next & MASK;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform full-width bit reversal in constant time using SWAR patterns, not per-bit loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit composition as hi/lo 64-bit
- SWAR bit-reversal: 1,2,4,8,16,32-bit block swaps
- Final cross-half swap to complete reversal

## Code Example
```javascript
// 64-bit reversal helper
static uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x = (x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL | (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1;
  x = (x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL | (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2;
  x = (x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL | (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4;
  x = (x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL | (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8;
  x = (x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL | (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16;
  x = (x >> 32) & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL | (x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32;
  return x;
}
uint128 reverse128(uint128 x){
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = reverse64(x.hi);
  r.hi = reverse64(x.lo);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to larger widths (256-bit) with minimal extra code?
- What intrinsics or compiler options help optimize this on typical CPUs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply SWAR-style bit counting across a 128-bit structure without branches, using two independent 64-bit halves. Also checks portability when no native 128-bit constant exists.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR popcount on 64-bit chunks
- 128-bit represented as { hi, lo }
- Edge-case handling for 0 and all-ones

## Code Example

```c
uint8_t popcount128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo;
  lo = lo - ((lo >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  lo = (lo & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((lo >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  lo = (lo + (lo >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  lo = lo + (lo >> 8);
  lo = lo + (lo >> 16);
  lo = lo + (lo >> 32);

  uint64_t hi = x.hi;
  hi = hi - ((hi >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  hi = (hi & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((hi >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  hi = (hi + (hi >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  hi = hi + (hi >> 8);
  hi = hi + (hi >> 16);
  hi = hi + (hi >> 32);

  uint8_t cl = lo & 0x7F;
  uint8_t ch = hi & 0x7F;
  return cl + ch;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for wider word sizes (e.g., 256-bit)?
- Could compiler intrinsics offer portable fallbacks here?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft branchless bit-twiddling in 128-bit space, a common low-level optimization task.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit integer handling via two 64-bit halves
- Trailing-zero count (ctz) tricks without branches
- De Bruijn sequences for fast bit indexing

## Code Example
```c
unsigned countTrailingZeros128(uint128 x) {
  static const unsigned char deBruijn64[64] = {
    0, 1, 2, 53, 3, 7, 54, 27,
    4, 38, 8, 34, 55, 48, 28, 20,
    5, 39, 53, 6, 59, 29, 42, 16,
    9, 35, 60, 50, 45, 27, 21, 17,
    10, 49, 56, 40, 30, 14, 43, 15,
    11, 23, 51, 52, 41, 19, 13, 44,
    12, 26, 58, 36, 31, 37, 29, 22
  };
  unsigned loIdx = ((x.lo & -x.lo) * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ull) >> 58;
  unsigned hiIdx = ((x.hi & -x.hi) * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ull) >> 58;
  unsigned tzLo = deBruijn64[loIdx];
  unsigned tzHi = deBruijn64[hiIdx];
  unsigned isLoZero = (x.lo == 0);
  return (1 - isLoZero) * tzLo + isLoZero * (64 + tzHi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support a variable-width 128-bit type with non-power-of-two lanes?
- Compare performance of this approach against a masked binary search approach on modern CPUs.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests endianness handling and 128-bit manipulation with no branches.

## Key Concepts
- 64-bit byte reversal
- 128-bit composition
- Branchless bit-twiddling

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverseBytes64(uint64_t x) {
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  x = (x << 32) | (x >> 32);
  return x;
}
uint128 mirrorBytes128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 y; y.hi = reverseBytes64(x.lo); y.lo = reverseBytes64(x.hi); return y;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for streaming data where only a portion is available?
- What are the performance trade-offs of inlining reverseBytes64?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to apply a classic bit-twiddling pattern (popcount) to a 128-bit type by handling two 64-bit halves, staying branchless and efficient.

## Key Concepts
- Hamming weight via masks and shifts
- Handling composite 128-bit types (hi/lo halves)
- Branchless aggregation of results

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef unsigned long long ull;
typedef struct { ull hi; ull lo; } uint128;

static inline unsigned long long popcount64(ull x){
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  return x & 0x7F;
}

unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x){
  return (unsigned int)(popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests precise understanding of bit-rotation, modulo-64 behavior, and edge cases without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Rotation via (x << s) | (x >> (64 - s)) with s = n & 63
- Safe when s = 0 using masking (64 - s) & 63
- No loops or conditionals; only shifts and ORs

## Code Example
```javascript
// JavaScript BigInt version for 64-bit rotation
function rotl64(x, n) {
  const s = BigInt(n & 63);
  const MASK = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFn;
  return ((x << s) | (x >> ((64n - s) & 63n))) & MASK;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 128-bit types?
- What compiler optimizations help ensure constants are folded efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests bitboard reasoning, edge handling, and efficient parallelism using only bitwise ops.

## Key Concepts
- Bitboard edge wrapping and file masks
- Parallel neighbor computation via multiple shifts
- Safe, branch-free bitwise design

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t neighborMask64(uint64_t board) {
    const uint64_t notA = 0xfefefefefefefefeULL;
    const uint64_t notH = 0x7f7f7f7f7f7f7f7fULL;
    uint64_t east  = (board & notH) << 1;
    uint64_t west  = (board & notA) >> 1;
    uint64_t south = board << 8;
    uint64_t north = board >> 8;
    uint64_t ne = (board & notH) >> 7;
    uint64_t nw = (board & notA) >> 9;
    uint64_t se = (board & notH) << 9;
    uint64_t sw = (board & notA) << 7;
    return east | west | south | north | ne | nw | se | sw;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for a 32-bit board layout?
- How would you unit-test edge cases (corners and edges) efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to implement a constant-time bit reversal to verify a binary palindrome using only bitwise ops.

## Key Concepts

- Palindrome property in binary
- Bit-reversal networks with masks
- Branch-free comparison

## Code Example

```javascript
// C-like implementation (illustrative)
int isBinaryPalindrome32(uint32_t n) {
  uint32_t x = n;
  x = ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555) | ((x & 0x55555555) << 1);
  x = ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333) | ((x & 0x33333333) << 2);
  x = ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F) | ((x & 0x0F0F0F0F) << 4);
  x = ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF) | ((x & 0x00FF00FF) << 8);
  x = (x >> 16) | (x << 16);
  return (x == n);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- Extend to 64-bit inputs; what changes? 
- Compare against a loop-based reversal for performance tests.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests low-level arithmetic with 128-bit values, ensuring correct carry propagation and branchless coding. It mirrors real-world bit-twiddling tasks in crypto, compression, or hash pipelines where performance and correctness matter.

## Key Concepts

- Carry propagation across 64-bit boundaries
- 128-bit arithmetic using hi/lo
- Branchless implementation using comparisons as carry indicators

## Code Example

```c
uint128 add128(uint128 a, uint128 b, unsigned *carryOut) {
  uint64_t lo = a.lo + b.lo;
  unsigned c0 = lo < a.lo;
  uint64_t hi = a.hi + b.hi + c0;
  unsigned c1 = hi < a.hi;
  *carryOut = c1;
  return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to subtraction with borrow?
- How would you verify correctness with randomized tests without branches?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of low-level bit hacks: packing, masking, and fixed-time transformations without control flow.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (Z-order) interleaving
- Bit spreading via staged masks
- Fixed, branchless implementation

## Code Example
```cpp
uint32_t spread16(uint16_t v){
  uint32_t x = v;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t interleave16(uint16_t a, uint16_t b){
  return spread16(a) | (spread16(b) << 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to interleave 32-bit values into a 64-bit result?
- What tests would you add to validate edge cases like a or b == 0xFFFF?
